WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.380
Although the majority of Marvel actors seem
to enjoy playing their roles in the franchise,

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:08.460
a number of stars have ended up pretty upset
enough with how their scenes or even whole

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.460
movie turned out.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.340
Here are a few of the things Marvel movie
actors have seriously regretted having to

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:13.840
do.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.620
For an actor who is known as much for his
muscular physique as he is for his acting,

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.660
it might come as a surprise to find that Chris
Hemsworth doesn't actually enjoy filming scenes

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.140
shirtless, especially if it wasn't in the
script.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:30.820
Speaking about his time on 2013's Thor: The
Dark World, Hemsworth told MTV that his shirtless

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.800
scene was mandated by Joss Whedon.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:33.660
Hemsworth said:

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.480
"He just said that there needed to be something
romantic in there.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.420
Then he said, 'Chris, get your shirt off.'

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:39.920
I said, 'Well, I don't know.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:40.780
What's the why?

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:41.280
What are we doing?'

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.620
The justification was that it was him coming
home from battle, washing blood off his hands.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:46.640
It is what it is."

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:50.460
"It's like his muscles are made of katati
metal fiber.”

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.240
“Stop massaging his muscles."

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.079
But he was even more upset during filming
on Thor: Ragnarok, since he felt including

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.960
yet another shirtless scene for Thor was verging
on becoming exploitative.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.060
Hemsworth said:

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.740
"This time around, there wasn't one in the
script, which I was really happy about.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.080
Then [director Taika Waititi said, 'No, I
think we need a shirt off scene.'

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.720
I was like, 'Ugh, no we've done that.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:12.920
Don't make me do it again!'

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.030
I just think it has to be different each time.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.140
You have an obligation to mix it up and advance
it in some way."

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.260
Avengers: Infinity War opens on the aftermath
of a fierce battle between the survivors of

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:27.490
Asgard's destruction and Thanos, as the heroes
unsuccessfully attempt to stop Thanos from

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.240
claiming the Tesseract from Loki.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:34.200
Loki's attempt to trick and kill Thanos fails,
however, and Thanos brutally chokes Loki to

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:34.700
death.

00:01:35.249 --> 00:01:39.760
Unlike Loki's many previous deaths, this one
actually sticks, and the sequence sets a bleak

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.689
tone for one of the darker installments in
the MCU.

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:45.000
"No resurrections this time."

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:49.560
For Thanos, killing the God of Mischief might
have been just another day at the office,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.440
but it was a good deal harder to process for
the man playing him.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.680
Josh Brolin later told USA Today.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.400
"He had been with that character for so long.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.080
And he's so lauded for doing it.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.080
Tom was so vulnerable at that moment.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.880
So choking him out wasn't the most fun thing
I have ever done."

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.060
In the earlier days of the MCU, Clint Barton
often came under fire by fans for being a

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.500
little vanilla in terms of character development.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:14.600
But Hawkeye actor Jeremy Renner had his own
problems when it came to the character's presence

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:15.460
in The Avengers.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.400
During the movie, Hawkeye has barely been
on-screen for a minute before he's put under

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.200
Loki's spell.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.540
Talking to GamesRadar, Renner later said:

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.080
"For 90% of the movie, I'm not the character
I signed on to play...

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.120
It's kind of a vacancy.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.070
[...] I was limited, you know what I mean?

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.519
I was a terminator in a way.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:31.020
Fun stunts.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.720
But is there any sort of emotional content
or thought process?

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:34.460
No."

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:39.840
Conveniently enough, Deadpool actor Ryan Reynolds
is one of the character's biggest fanboys

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:43.800
and was himself the producer who finally brought
the wisecracking assassin to the screen after

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:44.880
many years of trying.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.380
"Maximum effort."

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.290
But it's easy to forget that Reynolds played
Wade Wilson years before his solo film, with

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.020
his small role in X-Men Origins: Wolverine.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.739
As Reynolds told People magazine:

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.780
"It was a very frustrating experience.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.959
I was already attached to the Deadpool movie.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.840
We hadn't at that point written a script yet.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.760
[Origins] came along and it was sort of like,
'Play Deadpool in this movie or we'll get

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.540
someone else to.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.480
And I just said, 'I'll do it, but it's the
wrong version.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.700
Deadpool isn't correct in it."

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:16.319
To make matters worse, X-Men Origins was filming
during the writers' strike of 2007 and 2008,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:17.870
so Reynolds had to write his own lines.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:18.860
He explained:

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.160
"So we were in the middle of production, there
were no writers, no anything.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.500
Every line I have in the movie I just wrote
myself because in the script we had, it said,

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:29.060
'Wade Wilson shows up, talks really fast.'

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.060
I was like, 'What?

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.360
What am I supposed to do with that?'"

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:35.540
That's understandably frustrating, but let's
be honest: letting Ryan Reynolds ad lib his

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:38.260
way through the film may be the only thing
that made it watchable.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:39.799
"You look really nice today.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.860
It's the green - brings out the seriousness
in your eyes.”

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.360
“Oh my God, do you ever shut up, pal?

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.100
“No...not when I'm awake."

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:50.760
Like his predecessor in the role, Sir Ian
McKellan, Michael Fassbender brought a wealth

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:55.099
of stage presence to his portrayal of traumatized
Holocaust survivor Eric Lehnsherr and his

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.800
alter ego, the morally ambiguous mutant Magneto.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.400
Fassbender gave the character a whole new
level of humanity in X-Men: First Class.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.400
But by 2016's X-Men: Days of Future Past,
he was no longer happy with his work in the

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.540
role, and even became visibly embarrassed
when scenes from the film aired at the Toronto

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:12.720
International Film Festival.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.200
In particular, he hated the scene that saw
him and Professor X getting into an argument

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:17.700
in a plane.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:19.060
You, know, this scene:

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:20.750
"Where were you, Charles?

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.020
We were supposed to protect them.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.140
Where were you when your own people needed
you?"

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.000
Vulture reported Fassbender as saying:

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.400
"I don't actually like that performance there,
to be honest.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:31.919
I just think it's me shouting.

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:33.330
It's just like... some dude shouting."

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.710
The Fantastic Four franchise has struggled
with a series of setbacks and disappointments

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:42.940
over the years such as those experienced by
Jessica Alba, who almost quit acting after

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:47.200
playing Sue Storm in 2007's Fantastic Four:
Rise of the Silver Surfer.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.160
According to SyFy, Alba explained:

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.820
"The director was like, [...] 'Can you be
prettier when you cry?

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:53.550
[...] Don't do that thing with your face.

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:54.550
Just make it flat.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:55.860
We can CGI the tears in.'

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.140
[...] And then it got me thinking: Am I not
good enough?

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.620
Are my instincts and my emotions not good
enough?

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.640
Do people hate them so much that they don't
want me to be a person?

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.040
[...] And so I just said, 'F— it.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:07.200
I don't care about this business anymore.'"

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.220
Hugo Weaving has played a part in some of
the biggest movie franchises in history, including

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.500
Lord of the Rings, The Hobbit, and The Matrix
franchises.

00:05:15.009 --> 00:05:18.560
So it makes perfect sense that an actor of
his caliber would also find a place in the

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.660
Marvel Cinematic Universe which he quickly
did, when he played the villain Red Skull

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:24.490
in Captain America: The First Avenger.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.120
Unfortunately, while he was glad he gave it
a go, he didn't exactly enjoy the experience.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:30.480
Weaving told Collider:

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.500
"It's not something I would want to do again.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.860
I think I've done my dash with that sort of
film.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:38.800
It was good to do it and try it out, but to
be honest, it's not the sort of film I seek

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:40.560
out and really am excited by."

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:44.440
Weaving added that he signed a multi-picture
deal and said Marvel could have forced him

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:48.190
to reprise the role but also pointed out that
it would have been pretty silly to force a

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:50.780
performer to show up for a project they don't
want to be in.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:56.000
Mickey Rourke was an absolute scene-stealer
as Ivan Vanko in Iron Man 2.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.160
Rourke brought an underlying empathy and humanity
to the role that makes his performance incredibly

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.560
compelling, but he told ScreenRant that the
way Vanko was written was far too one-dimensional

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:05.340
for his taste.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:06.440
He explained:

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.540
"The bad guy doesn't always have to be one-dimensional,
you know?

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.780
[...] The performance I tried to bring to
[Vanko] ends up on the f---ing floor.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.699
That could cause you not to care as much not
to want to put that effort in when you try

00:06:16.699 --> 00:06:20.580
to make it an intelligent bad guy [...] This
character was hard because he was written

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:21.580
as pure evil."

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.680
Unlike many of his colleagues who love being
part of the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Idris

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:31.040
Elba has told The Telegraph that playing Heimdall
in the MCU movies quickly became a chore for

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:31.540
him.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.180
After spending eight months in South Africa
making the Nelson Mandela biopic Mandela,

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:40.860
Elba added that coming back to shoot scenes
for Thor: The Dark World was "torture," explaining:

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:44.740
"I'm actually falling down from a spaceship,
so they had to put me in [a] harness in this

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:45.819
green-screen studio.

00:06:45.819 --> 00:06:50.449
And in between takes I was stuck there, fake
hair stuck on to my head with glue, this f---ing

00:06:50.449 --> 00:06:51.720
helmet, while they reset.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.180
And I'm thinking: '24 hours ago, I was Mandela.'

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.460
Then there I was, in this stupid harness,
with this wig and this sword and these contact

00:06:58.460 --> 00:06:58.960
lenses.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:06:59.960
It ripped my heart out."

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.620
Tobey Maguire and Andrew Garfield both may
have played Spider-Man within a few years

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:08.340
of each other, but only the latter came out
of the experience seriously unhappy.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:11.880
Among other regrets, Garfield said that he
thought his scenes and character development

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:12.880
were all wrong.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.600
"I signed up to this story and this incredible
character that I’ve been dressing as since

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:18.260
I was three."

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:23.220
"It gets compromised and it breaks your…
breaks my heart."

00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:26.990
Garfield blamed this misuse of the character
on the big-budget nature of the movies as

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.760
well as the capitalist machine that is more
interested in making money than content.

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:37.300
"I was still young enough to struggle with
the uh… the value system I suppose of of

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:39.139
um..

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:40.820
Of ah corporate America really."

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:45.479
Garfield soon exited the role after a pair
of films and was replaced by another British

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:49.040
actor, Tom Holland, who took up the web-shooters
in Captain America: Civil War.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:54.040
Marvel took a long time trying to find a version
of Hulk that worked for audiences and critics

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:57.940
alike, before finally hitting paydirt with
Mark Ruffalo's now-iconic portrayal of the

00:07:57.940 --> 00:07:58.440
character.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:02.200
"Thor, I think I'm freaking out."

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:06.229
Before Ruffalo took the part, however, the
studio suffered a serious disappointment with

00:08:06.229 --> 00:08:10.220
The Incredible Hulk in 2008, which starred
Edward Norton as the title character.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:14.680
A few years after leaving the MCU, Norton
told the audience at Bruce Willis' Comedy

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:18.880
Central roast that he had encountered problems
on the set because he, quote, "wanted a better

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:19.380
script."

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:23.620
"I thought maybe we should try to make one
Marvel movie that was at least as good as

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:26.729
the worst Chris Nolan movie, but what the
hell was I thinking?"

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:30.600
He has also claimed that he was invited to
help improve the script but Marvel reneged;

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.100
either way, the end result failed to live
up to expectations.

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:38.350
The bad blood between Marvel and its erstwhile
Hulk actor is apparently mutual, too.

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:41.520
In a statement prior to the release of The
Avengers, Marvel announced:

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:45.540
"We have made the decision to not bring Ed
Norton back to portray the title role of Bruce

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:46.040
Banner.

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:50.600
Our decision is definitely not one based on
monetary factors, but instead rooted in the

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:54.700
need for an actor who embodies the creativity
and collaborative spirit of our other talented

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:55.480
cast members."

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:00.900
Long before he played Batman in the DCEU,
Ben Affleck starred in one of the lesser-loved

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:06.180
Marvel adaptations as lawyer-by-day, vigilante-by-night
Matt Murdock — also known as Daredevil.

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:10.640
Unlike some of the other Marvel-inspired bombs
that later turned into cult classics, Affleck's

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:13.980
Daredevil has yet to find any kind of adoration
from Marvel fans.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:17.970
In fact, it's mostly been forgotten… but
its star doesn't mind that one bit.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:19.890
Speaking to Playboy magazine, affleck said:

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.410
"The only movie I actually regret is Daredevil.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:23.180
It just kills me.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.920
I love that story, that character, and the
fact that it got f---ed up the way it did

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:27.640
stays with me.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.080
Maybe that's part of the motivation to do
Batman."

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.640
Unfortunately, when he was announced as the
new Batman, it was his performance in Daredevil

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.480
that had fans concerned about his ability
to pull off the role.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.010
At the time, the actor said:

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:43.060
"If I thought the result would be another
Daredevil, I'd be out there picketing myself.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.820
Why would I make the movie if I didn't think
it was going to be good?"

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.480
Long before Howard the Duck made his brief
cameos in the Guardians of the Galaxy movies,

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:54.240
he made his full-length feature film debut
in 1986's Howard the Duck.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:58.540
This was the story of a parallel universe
where an alien anthropomorphic duck is brought

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:02.900
to Earth after an accident on his planet and
the whole thing turned out a little raunchier

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:04.160
than most Marvel movies.

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:06.780
"No more MISTER NICE DUCK"

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:10.660
When asked about her role as Howard's love
interest Beverly in Howard the Duck, star

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:12.620
Lea Thompson told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:14.440
"I was the number one star of it.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:15.300
It was painful."

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:19.340
Among many of the truly bizarre things that
happen in the movie, Howard the Duck also

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:23.930
features an intimate scene between Thompson
and Howard, which is as graphic as innuendo

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.600
can get without veering straight into bestiality.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.780
Is it any wonder she didn't enjoy it?

